WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.733 --> 00:00:01.567
DERRICA WILSON:<i> This little girl</i>

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:04.504
<i>had just gotten off the metro
 with her mom.</i>

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:06.605
<i>We were passing out flyers.</i>

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:09.275
<i> She just stopped.
 She was staring.</i>

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:11.010
I asked, "Do you know her?"

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:15.615
<i>She looks up at me and she says,
"She looks just like me."</i>

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:17.917
Every time I look
at this picture,

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:19.018
it makes me emotional.

00:00:19.084 --> 00:00:21.420
♪ (SOLEMN MUSIC PLAYING) ♪

00:00:21.487 --> 00:00:25.224
NATALIE WILSON.
<i> There's so many missing
people of color in the US.</i>

00:00:25.791 --> 00:00:29.695
<i>African Americans remain missing
 four times longer</i>

00:00:29.828 --> 00:00:30.930
<i> than White Americans.</i>

00:00:30.996 --> 00:00:33.299
That number is alarming.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.668
NATALIE:<i> We hear from families
 all the time,</i>

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:39.172
<i>"The police is turning us away,
the media wouldn't respond."</i>

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:40.473
I'm looking for
our missing daughter.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.742
DERRICA:<i> And it drove me
and my sister-in-law, Natalie,</i>

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:46.980
<i>to wanna do something about it
to help these other families.</i>

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.782
<i> Because, if not us, who?</i>

00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:52.785
NATALIE:<i> It's our job
 to bring them home.</i>

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:53.886
(PHONE DIAL TONE)

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:56.922
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i> Thank you for calling
Black and Missing Foundation.</i>

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:57.957
SPEAKER:<i> The police was like,</i>

00:00:58.123 --> 00:00:59.225
<i> "How you know she
 just ain't leave?"</i>

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.961
SPEAKER 2:<i> The detective
kept saying, "She ran away."</i>

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.529
And I'm like,
"She didn't run away."

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.264
<i>It was like a punch in the face.</i>

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:09.335
When a Black person is
in distress, missing,

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:12.238
<i> it's not a big deal
 to law enforcement</i>

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:15.308
because they don't think
we have much to lose.

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:16.943
This one always gets me.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.711
Just remembering
when she was a little girl.

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:20.680
<i> You blame yourself
because it's never enough.</i>

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:26.786
My sister went missing in 2009.
Of all the major news stations,
nobody would talk to me.

00:01:26.852 --> 00:01:30.456
Gwen Ifill coined the term
missing white woman syndrome.

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:33.593
If you don't
meet those criteria,

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:37.530
<i> blonde hair, blue eyes,
your stories are not newsworthy.</i>

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:41.200
SPEAKER 3:
Here's my niece. She's missing.
Her story is just as compelling.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.304
SPEAKER 4:<i> We have nothing
 for our missing people</i>

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.672
<i> except Black folk's spit
 and grit.</i>

00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:48.941
That's why I'm on ten right now.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:51.544
And this is not one and done.
We will be back.

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:56.782
DERRICA:<i> We don't want families
 to ever give up on hope.</i>

00:01:56.982 --> 00:02:00.854
NATALIE:
<i> One year, five, ten, 20.
We have seen miracles happen.</i>

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.089
DERRICA:<i> These men and women
 that are going missing</i>

00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:06.059
<i> look like my siblings
 or look like my parents</i>

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.495
<i> and my grandparents.
 They matter.</i>

00:02:09.795 --> 00:02:11.798
<i> Our community matters.</i>

